#: ../../../guides/introducing_black_to_your_project.md:1
msgid "Introducing _Black_ to your project"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/introducing_black_to_your_project.md:3
msgid "Tips for existing projects"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/introducing_black_to_your_project.md:5
msgid ""
"There is no single way to introduce _Black_ to an existing project. The "
"right approach depends on the size of the codebase, active development, and "
"the team's review workflow. The following tips can help keep the change "
"manageable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/introducing_black_to_your_project.md:9
msgid ""
"Agreeing on which files to format and recording options (such as target "
"Python versions and line length) in `pyproject.toml` can make the initial "
"reformat easier to review. Pinning the same _Black_ version in local "
"development and CI also helps contributors produce consistent results."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/introducing_black_to_your_project.md:14
msgid ""
"If the project already has established string conventions, using `--skip-"
"string-normalization` can reduce churn during the initial adoption. Treat "
"this as an adoption aid rather than a recommended default for new projects, "
"where _Black_'s standard string normalization keeps formatting consistent."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/introducing_black_to_your_project.md:19
msgid ""
"Running the project's tests before and after formatting helps check for "
"unexpected changes. Keeping the formatting in its own commit, without "
"refactors or behavior changes, makes review and future history easier to "
"follow. A single reformat may suit a small project; for a larger one, "
"formatting one directory or package at a time may be easier to coordinate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/introducing_black_to_your_project.md:25
msgid ""
"To keep new changes formatted, consider adding `black --check .` to CI and "
"using the {doc}`pre-commit integration </integrations/pre-commit>` for local "
"feedback. Configuration, formatting, and enforcement can be reviewed "
"together or in separate pull requests. If you want to ignore the formatting "
"commit in `git blame`, choose a merge method that preserves that commit "
"separately from other changes. Separate pull requests are one option for "
"projects that use squash merges."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/introducing_black_to_your_project.md:32
msgid "Coordinating active branches"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/introducing_black_to_your_project.md:34
msgid ""
"A repository-wide formatting commit can conflict with long-running branches "
"that edit the same lines. Announcing the planned merge time can help "
"contributors coordinate changes around it. Once the formatting commit is on "
"the target branch, contributors can update their branch, resolve any "
"semantic conflicts, and run _Black_ over the result. The final diff against "
"the newly formatted target branch will then mostly contain the intended code "
"changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/introducing_black_to_your_project.md:41
msgid ""
"Avoid combining the reformat with automatic conflict strategies that always "
"choose one side: they can silently discard real changes. For especially busy "
"repositories, merging small or nearly complete branches before the rollout "
"can reduce the number of conflicts considerably."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/introducing_black_to_your_project.md:46
msgid "Avoiding ruining git blame"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/introducing_black_to_your_project.md:48
msgid ""
"A long-standing argument against moving to automated code formatters like "
"_Black_ is that the migration will clutter up the output of `git blame`. "
"This was a valid argument, but since Git version 2.23, Git natively supports "
"[ignoring revisions in blame](https://git-scm.com/docs/git-"
"blame#Documentation/git-blame.txt---ignore-revltrevgt) with the `--ignore-"
"rev` option. You can also pass a file listing the revisions to ignore using "
"the `--ignore-revs-file` option. The changes made by the revision will be "
"ignored when assigning blame. Lines modified by an ignored revision will be "
"blamed on the previous revision that modified those lines."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/introducing_black_to_your_project.md:57
msgid ""
"So when migrating your project's code style to _Black_, reformat everything "
"and commit the changes (preferably in one massive commit). Then put the full "
"40 characters commit identifier(s) into a file usually called `.git-blame-"
"ignore-revs` at the root of your project directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/introducing_black_to_your_project.md:62
msgid ""
"# Migrate code style to Black\n"
"5b4ab991dede475d393e9d69ec388fd6bd949699\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/introducing_black_to_your_project.md:67
msgid ""
"Afterwards, you can pass that file to `git blame` and see clean and "
"meaningful blame information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/introducing_black_to_your_project.md:70
msgid ""
"$ git blame important.py --ignore-revs-file .git-blame-ignore-revs\n"
"7a1ae265 (John Smith 2019-04-15 15:55:13 -0400 1) def "
"very_important_function(text, file):\n"
"abdfd8b0 (Alice Doe  2019-09-23 11:39:32 -0400 2)     text = text.lstrip()\n"
"7a1ae265 (John Smith 2019-04-15 15:55:13 -0400 3)     with open(file, \"r+"
"\") as f:\n"
"7a1ae265 (John Smith 2019-04-15 15:55:13 -0400 4)         "
"f.write(formatted)\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/introducing_black_to_your_project.md:78
msgid ""
"You can even configure `git` to automatically ignore revisions listed in a "
"file on every call to `git blame`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/introducing_black_to_your_project.md:81
msgid "$ git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guides/introducing_black_to_your_project.md:85
msgid ""
"**The one caveat is that some online Git-repositories do not yet support "
"ignoring revisions using their native blame UI.** So blame information will "
"be cluttered with a reformatting commit on those platforms. However, [GitHub]"
"(https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/working-with-files/using-files/"
"viewing-a-file#ignore-commits-in-the-blame-view) and [GitLab (since version "
"17.10)](https://about.gitlab.com/releases/2025/03/20/gitlab-17-10-released/"
"#ignore-specific-revisions-in-git-blame) both support `.git-blame-ignore-"
"revs` in blame views by default."
msgstr ""
